[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: web_server_1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: The Apache HTTP service on web_server_1 is misconfigured for TLS. The HTTPS (port 443) listener is not present, so only plain HTTP (port 80) is served.

Mechanism: From host_1_1_1_1, HTTP to web1.local succeeds (HTTP/1.1 200 OK), but HTTPS connections to web1.local/10.200.0.4 are refused. Browsers that use an https bookmark or auto-upgrade to HTTPS will spin and then error out, which the user perceives as a timeout, while HTTP works fine.

Localization: On web_server_1, ss/netstat shows only 0.0.0.0:80 listening, no 443. The loaded Apache modules do not include ssl_module, and there is no SSL virtual host configured (no SSLEngine/SSLCertificate directives found). On the client, DNS for web1.local resolves correctly to 10.200.0.4, and network reachability/ping is healthy.

Root cause: Apache on web_server_1 is not configured to serve HTTPS (mod_ssl is not loaded and there is no :443 vhost), so TLS connections to web1.local are refused.

Corrective fix: Enable and configure HTTPS on web_server_1. Concretely: a2enmod ssl; create/enable a :443 virtual host for web1.local (e.g., a2ensite default-ssl or a proper vhost), install a valid certificate/key for web1.local, and reload Apache (systemctl reload apache2). As a temporary workaround, instruct users to use http://web1.local, but the durable fix is to bring port 443 online with TLS. Evidence: client curl -I http://web1.local => 200 OK; curl -vkI https://10.200.0.4 => “connect to 10.200.0.4 port 443 failed: Connection refused”; web_server_1 ss shows LISTEN on :80 only; apache2ctl -M shows no ssl_module.
=== END ===